  "definition": "A bilateral squat facing a fixed landmine pivot, with both hands holding the moving sleeve in front of the chest throughout the repetition. Both feet remain planted while the hips and knees flex to a controlled, self-selected depth, then extend to standing as the sleeve follows its pivoted arc. ACE names the landmine goblet squat and Collins et al. directly studied the landmine-squat category, but the accessible study abstract does not establish grip, depth, or joint kinematics. The exact two-hand chest hold and controlled depth are a bounded catalog fixture; the categorical joint actions and muscle roles are anatomy-and-mechanics inferences, not measured rankings. A press, alternating-hand release, rotation, lunge, machine-guided squat, or free-bar squat is outside this family. Log added plates only and compare loads only on the same bar and pivot setup.",
